<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">1st Proletarian Brigade</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 1st Proletarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> was the first regular brigade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-partisans">Yugoslav Partisans</a>, formed on 21 December 1941 at Rudo in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> from units that had retreated from the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzice">Užice</a>. Its formation marked the shift from local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> detachments to a mobile army. It fought through the war in Bosnia, Montenegro and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and took part in the liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/1st-proletarian-brigade.jpg" alt="1st Proletarian Brigade" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tito_predaje_zastavu_Prvoj_proleterskoj_brigadi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The brigade was formed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a> and the Central Committee of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in the village of Rudo, the first brigade-size formation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-partisans">Yugoslav Partisans</a>. At its formation it numbered 1,186 soldiers, NCOs and officers, 71 of them women, drawn from the best Serb and Montenegrin Partisans, under the command of Koča Popović, a veteran of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>. It took on a more multi-ethnic character from March 1942, when 15 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosniaks">Bosniaks</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a> joined it.</p><p>It gave the Partisan Supreme Command a politically reliable, mobile force not tied to any one district, so that fighting power could be concentrated at a chosen point and withdrawn in the face of a stronger enemy. It fought its first battle the day after its creation, against Italian troops and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chetniks">Chetniks</a> as it marched out of Rudo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During Operation Southeast Croatia the brigade crossed Mount Igman in freezing temperatures, and after Operation Trio it took part in the Partisan Long March from Zelengora in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> to the west. Its withdrawal from eastern Bosnia to Foča led to recriminations between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> and Svetozar Vukmanović. It was an independent brigade until June 1942, then part of the Strike Group of Proletarian Brigades, and from November 1942 it became the 1st Proletarian Division.</p><p>It fought the Italians, the Germans and their Chetnik auxiliaries and the forces of the Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, and took part in six of the Seven Enemy Offensives of Yugoslav historiography. For most of the war it relied on manoeuvre and initiative, marching some 20,000 km, until after the Belgrade Offensive it fought frontal battles on the Sremski front. In 41 months of fighting 13,443 men and 651 women served in it, and 4,818 of them died in battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>It was counted among the elite formations of the National Liberation Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, and its enemies respected it: in his war journal in 1944, Artur Phleps, commander of the V SS Mountain Corps, judged it a serious, well-led and well-trained opponent that fought like regular troops. In 1971 the Museum of the First Proletarian Brigade was opened at Rudo to commemorate the unit.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “1st Proletarian Brigade” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A German offensive in late November 1941 retook Užice, and the Partisans withdrew into the Sandžak and eastern Bosnia, where they formed the <b>First Proletarian Brigade</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-uzice">Republic of Užice</a></li></ul></section>
